{"uuid":"54D3937B-C92F-5483-A127-43B2986429AF","name":"Teriyaki Chicken","serves":10,"duration":15,"cookingDuration":210,"defaultScale":1,"isPublicRecipe":false,"folderIDs":[],"ingredients":[{"uuid":"704CB778-20C7-5AA2-9E50-D10440C13CCF","order":0,"ingredient":{"uuid":"6267B6EE-3B95-5677-A006-DCF72AC0BF9D","name":"chicken breast, whole (not cubed)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"POUND","amount":4.5}},{"uuid":"825CBF58-9042-568A-832E-FB5932F840B6","order":1,"ingredient":{"uuid":"97510C68-24DF-5489-99C1-7DFE5705687B","name":"chicken broth"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"CUP","amount":0.5}},{"uuid":"D0734C61-9BC4-5F9A-8F5C-2E8E34A22DDE","order":2,"ingredient":{"uuid":"AA47E063-22A0-596A-90E0-655F127B5656","name":"jasmine rice (dry)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"CUP","amount":3.5}},{"uuid":"0319567F-2BA1-5421-9E25-CFFEC7A997B5","order":3,"ingredient":{"uuid":"92CB2AF5-8E39-5C75-A657-7EAA180E80AF","name":"broccoli florets (yields ~1200 g roasted)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"POUND","amount":5.5}},{"uuid":"56FC8DE4-D0D9-5E5A-8260-6CA366C08E0D","order":4,"ingredient":{"uuid":"EE00356D-8621-51AF-B0C3-D134C075B961","name":"bottles teriyaki sauce (~422 g total)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"ITEM","amount":2}},{"uuid":"1CE034F3-EE4D-510B-BF94-6C0EA7169D08","order":5,"ingredient":{"uuid":"07501558-0B84-5BE2-9346-28BF7F332C17","name":"Olive oil, salt, pepper"}}],"steps":[{"uuid":"E9531496-0846-5FB4-9EC7-6A9132829EDE","order":0,"step":"Place 4 ½ lbs whole chicken breasts in the crockpot. Season with salt and pepper and add ½ cup chicken broth for moisture. No cutting required.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"3E8D70B2-1313-5FFD-945F-B0B6625A8EA3","order":1,"step":"Cook on High 3–4 hours, or Low 6–7 hours, until the chicken reaches 165°F and is fork-tender.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"5B960096-2965-5D43-B0A9-EC2D2C30B0C9","order":2,"step":"Drain the cooking liquid. Draining prevents a watery result once the sauce goes on.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"8088DCE2-4CF5-5B0E-BBE5-A874DF67AAD8","order":3,"step":"Shred the chicken with a hand mixer directly in the crockpot — this takes about 30 seconds. A few pulses is enough; over-mixing turns it mushy.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"7F652A94-0D4B-5683-808E-70989AF42369","order":4,"step":"During the last ~20 minutes of chicken time, rinse 3 ½ cups jasmine rice 2–3 times until the water runs mostly clear. Add to the Instant Pot with 3 ½ cups water (1:1).","isSection":false},{"uuid":"DC360E47-FB8E-52FC-83EA-D19A267721EC","order":5,"step":"Confirm the valve is set to SEALING. Pressure Cook (Manual) on High for 3 minutes, natural release 10 minutes, then quick release. Fluff and leave the lid off to cool.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"75C246E1-0F6A-54CB-97E3-8F6628A765D3","order":6,"step":"Toss the broccoli with olive oil, salt, and pepper. Air roast at 400°F in the Ninja toaster oven for 12–15 minutes until the edges are slightly crispy. Do this in 2 batches — 5 ½ lbs will not fit in a single layer.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"E012E914-2BE3-5982-B618-7070242D3700","order":7,"step":"Let the chicken, rice, and broccoli cool 10–15 minutes before portioning. Spread the rice on a sheet pan to cool it faster.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"0950BDE0-8DAE-51EA-B30E-5AF2CED87B74","order":8,"step":"Assembly line across 10 open containers: 145 g chicken, then 140 g rice, then 120 g broccoli.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"8AF3B75F-3432-5F81-BC15-93565431F892","order":9,"step":"Portion ~42 g teriyaki sauce into 10 separate small containers. The sauce is packed separately here — adding it before storage makes the rice soggy by day 2.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"21A7845F-1B3D-5FBC-84D5-4D51919E8511","order":10,"step":"Label with a dry-erase marker. 6 containers to the fridge, 4 to the freezer. Move freezer containers to the fridge the night before eating.","isSection":false}],"notes":"UNTESTED — v2, never cooked\nQuantities are planned, not observed. Weigh the components and update the recipe before trusting these numbers.\n\nPER CONTAINER (10 total)\n  chicken: 145 g\n  rice: 140 g\n  broccoli: 120 g\n  sauce: 42 g\n  total: 447 g\n\nTAGS: no-cutting, freezer-friendly, hands-off, sauce-packed-separately, bottled-sauce\n\nSTORAGE: 6 fridge, 4 freezer\nFreezer containers move to the fridge the night before eating.\n\nUNTESTED IN THIS FORM — v2 moved this recipe from cubed oven-roasted chicken\nto whole breasts in the crockpot. The quantities below are planned against\n`docs/standing-parameters.md`, not observed for this version. Weigh the\nshredded chicken after step 4, the cooked rice after step 6, and the roasted\nbroccoli after step 7, then promote with a real date.\n\nWhy the switch. An undated session log of the cubed version measured:\n\n  Chicken    5.31 lbs raw → 984 g cooked   (yield 0.409)\n  Broccoli   5.68 lbs raw → 1240 g roasted (yield 0.48)\n  Rice       3 cups dry   → 1220 g cooked  (~407 g per dry cup)\n  Sauce      422 g total  → ~42 g each\n\nCubed chicken roasted at 425°F came in at 0.409 — the bottom of the 0.41–0.58\nrange, not the ~0.58 an earlier write-up assumed. That session portioned 98 g\nper container against a 145 g target, roughly 30 g of protein rather than 45.\nHitting 145 g at that yield would mean buying 7.8 lbs.\n\nWhole breasts in the crockpot plan at 0.72, so the same 1450 g of cooked meat\nneeds about 4 ½ lbs — a little over half the chicken, and no raw cubing. The\ntrade-off is shredded rather than cubed, and no seared edges; the sauce\ncarries it. This is the default standing parameters already recommends.\n\nBroccoli was the other correction. Standing parameters used to claim 3 ½–4 lbs\nraw gives ~1200 g roasted. Measured yield is 0.48, so 1200 g takes ~5 ½ lbs.\nThe old number under-buys by about 40%, which is why this recipe now calls for\n5 ½ lbs and two batches in the toaster oven.\n\nThe session log noted \"~50 g each\" for sauce while also recording 422 g total,\nwhich only divides to 42 g across 10 containers. 42 g is what the arithmetic\nsupports; if 50 g is what it actually takes to coat a bowl, that is a third\nbottle and about 500 mg more sodium per container. Worth settling on the next\ncook.\n\nSodium remains the problem. At roughly 1400 mg per container this is the worst\nin the rotation and nearly double the 800 mg ceiling, essentially all of it\nfrom the bottled sauce — the crockpot switch does not change that. The honey\ngarlic recipe is the worked example of the fix: a scratch sauce there lands at\n700 mg. A scratch teriyaki of soy, mirin, ginger, garlic, and a cornstarch\nslurry would do the same here.\n\nSauce is packed separately in this recipe, unlike the others where it is\nalready in the meat. Shredded chicken absorbs sauce well, so stirring it in\nduring a Warm hold is an option — but it was kept separate to protect the rice\nfrom going soggy. Add it after reheating.\n\nReheat in the air fryer at 350°F for 5–7 minutes. Rice and chicken freeze\nwell; broccoli softens slightly but holds.","neutritionalInfo":"Serving Size: 1 container,\nCalories: 540 kcal,\nProtein: 52 g,\nCarbohydrates: 60 g,\nFat: 11 g,\nFiber: 4 g,\nSodium: 1400 mg,\nSugar: 12 g","sourceName":"ten-containers","webLink":"https://zjbecker32.github.io/ten-containers/recipes/teriyaki-chicken/","tags":[],"images":[]}